---
import DocsLayout from '@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import { DocsTable, Callout } from '@/molecules/DocComponents/DocComponents';

const ROLES = [
  ['NEXUS', 'Orchestrator', 'Ingests issues, assigns work, coordinates the team, performs recovery, escalates to humans.', 'Yes \u2014 plans before acting'],
  ['FORGE', 'Builder', 'Plans (then halts), implements the approved plan, runs tests, opens pull requests.', 'No \u2014 but bounded by gates'],
  ['SENTINEL', 'Reviewer', 'Adversarially reviews the plan and the pull request; approves gates; requests evidence.', 'Yes \u2014 reviews before approving'],
  ['VESSEL', 'DevOps', 'Watches CI, resolves merge conflicts, merges approved pull requests, tears down workspaces.', 'No \u2014 but bounded by gates'],
  ['LORE', 'Writer', 'Documents decisions, maintains changelogs and history (often disabled by default).', 'No \u2014 but bounded by gates'],
];
---

<DocsLayout breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Docs', href: '/docs' }, { label: 'Architecture', href: '/docs/architecture' }, { label: 'Agent Roles' }]}>
  <h1>Agent Roles</h1>
  <p>
    OpenFlows runs a group of AI agents that cooperate like a real engineering team. Each agent has one primary job,
    and together they close the loop from issue to merged pull request. This page is the quick lookup for each role:
    who it is, what it does, and whether it must get approval before acting.
  </p>

  <h2>The five roles</h2>
  <DocsTable headers={['Role', 'Name', 'Responsibility', 'Requires approval to act?']} rows={ROLES} />

  <h2>What "requires approval to act" means</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Plan-mode roles</strong> (NEXUS, SENTINEL) are designed to review and gate rather than blindly proceed.</li>
    <li><strong>Builder / DevOps / Writer roles</strong> act directly, but their actions are bounded by gates: FORGE can't build without an approved plan, VESSEL won't merge an unapproved pull request, LORE only documents merged work.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The hand-offs between roles</h2>
  <p>
    The team flows in a fixed order: NEXUS plans the assignment → FORGE builds → SENTINEL reviews →
    VESSEL merges → LORE documents → back to NEXUS. Each transition only happens when the previous step
    produced the required result — an approved plan, a reviewed pull request, a successful merge.
  </p>
  <p>The team is sequential where it must be and parallel where it can be:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>NEXUS plans the assignment</strong> and hands work to an idle builder.</li>
    <li><strong>FORGE writes and stops</strong> until its plan is approved.</li>
    <li><strong>SENTINEL reviews</strong> — but never touches FORGE's workspace.</li>
    <li><strong>VESSEL merges</strong> and tears down the workspace.</li>
    <li><strong>LORE documents</strong> the completed work.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Roles are configured, not hard-coded</h2>
  <p>
    Which roles exist, how many workers each role can run at once, what model each uses, and what skills and tools are
    available — all of this lives in the <strong>agent registry</strong> in the control plane and can be changed
    without a restart or redeploy.
  </p>

  <h2>What an agent workspace looks like</h2>
  <p>Each agent runs in its own isolated, disposable workspace:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>No LLM API keys.</li>
    <li>No raw repository credentials.</li>
    <li>Heavily restricted network access (control plane, GitHub, coordination store — and little else).</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The agent coordinates with the team strictly through a small, typed command surface — it never talks directly
    to the coordination store or the machine plane. This is what keeps the whole system safe and auditable.
  </p>

  <Callout type="info" title="A team that extends without code">
    You can extend what the team can do <strong>without writing code</strong> by adding skills, tools, or models
    through the registry. Adding a brand-new <em>role</em> is the one extension that touches the system internally.
  </Callout>
</DocsLayout>
